    "question": {
      ":question/id": "bf67c2cc-23d0-55d5-b111-13d00cf98120",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186083243/review-0-policy-2-da51d3ee0942346e/assets/curve-79cdafe14b92f0f8a10a36ac.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=3 \\cos {t},\\,y=5 \\sin {2 t},\\,0\\le t<2\\pi,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{11 \\pi}{6}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=- 3 \\sin {t},\\,y'(t)=10 \\cos {2 t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(10 \\cos {2 t}\\right)}{\\left(- 3 \\sin {t}\\right)} \\\\  & =- \\frac{10 \\cos {2 t}}{3 \\sin {t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{11 \\pi}{6},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =- \\frac{10 \\cos {2 \\left(\\frac{11 \\pi}{6}\\right)}}{3 \\sin {\\left(\\frac{11 \\pi}{6}\\right)}} \\\\  & =- \\frac{10 \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{3 \\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
