      ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=- 4 \\sin {t},\\,y'(t)=3 \\cos {3 t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(3 \\cos {3 t}\\right)}{\\left(- 4 \\sin {t}\\right)} \\\\  & =- \\frac{3 \\cos {3 t}}{4 \\sin {t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{3 \\pi}{4},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =- \\frac{3 \\cos {3 \\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)}}{4 \\sin {\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}\\right)}} \\\\  & =- \\frac{3 \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}{4 \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =- \\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
