      ":question/problem": "![](images/7a/7ae65c873838ede4cce89c953ff2654fa1e58e65fdc87896c8ba85bbf947be20.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=6 \\cos {t},\\,y=2 \\sin {3 t},\\,0\\le t<2\\pi,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=- 6 \\sin {t},\\,y'(t)=6 \\cos {3 t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(6 \\cos {3 t}\\right)}{\\left(- 6 \\sin {t}\\right)} \\\\  & =- \\frac{\\cos {3 t}}{\\sin {t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{4 \\pi}{3},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t=\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}} & =- \\frac{\\cos {3 \\left(\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\right)}}{\\sin {\\left(\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}\\right)}} \\\\  & =- \\frac{\\left(1\\right)}{\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
