    "question": {
      ":question/id": "bdd8addd-19c9-5a6a-9086-bac2aa28ed0f",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186083243/review-0-policy-2-da51d3ee0942346e/assets/curve-a5012b952be2d6b7b076344f.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=4 \\sin {t},\\,y=4 \\cos {t},\\,0\\le t<2\\pi,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{5 \\pi}{4}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=4 \\cos {t},\\,y'(t)=- 4 \\sin {t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(- 4 \\sin {t}\\right)}{\\left(4 \\cos {t}\\right)} \\\\  & =- \\tan {t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{5 \\pi}{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t=\\frac{5 \\pi}{4}} & =- \\tan {\\left(\\frac{5 \\pi}{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =- \\left(1\\right) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
