      ":question/problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186083243/review-0-policy-2-da51d3ee0942346e/assets/curve-20b624c27bdffba01227643b.png)\n\nFor the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=e^{t},\\,y=\\ln{\\left(t + 1 \\right)},\\,-1<t<\\infty,\n$$\n\nshown above, calculate the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=e^{t},\\,y'(t)=\\frac{1}{t + 1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{t + 1}\\right)}{\\left(e^{t}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{e^{- t}}{t + 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1,$ the slope $m$ of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{dy}{dx}\\,{|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{e^{- \\left(1\\right)}}{\\left(1\\right) + 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2 e} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2 e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
